(b) (b) The following are appropriate supplier verification activities for raw materials and other ingredients:
(b)(1) (1) Onsite audits;
(b)(2) (2) Sampling and testing of the raw material or other ingredient;
(b)(3) (3) Review of the supplier's relevant food safety records; and
(b)(4) (4) Other appropriate supplier verification activities based on supplier performance and the risk associated with the raw material or other ingredient.
(c) (c) The supply-chain program must provide assurance that a hazard requiring a supply-chain-applied control has been significantly minimized or prevented.
(d)(1) (d)(1) Except as provided by paragraph (d)(2) of this section, in approving suppliers and determining the appropriate supplier verification activities and the frequency with which they are conducted, the following must be considered:
(d)(1)(i) (i) The hazard analysis of the food, including the nature of the hazard controlled before receipt of the raw material or other ingredient, applicable to the raw material and other ingredients;
(d)(1)(ii) (ii) The entity or entities that will be applying controls for the hazards requiring a supply-chain-applied control;
(d)(1)(iii) (iii) Supplier performance, including:
(d)(1)(iii)(A) (A) The supplier's procedures, processes, and practices related to the safety of the raw material and other ingredients;
(d)(1)(iii)(B) (B) Applicable FDA food safety regulations and information relevant to the supplier's compliance with those regulations, including an FDA warning letter or import alert relating to the safety of food and other FDA compliance actions related to food safety (or, when applicable, relevant laws and regulations of a country whose food safety system FDA has officially recognized as comparable or has determined to be equivalent to that of the United States, and information relevant to the supplier's compliance with those laws and regulations); and
